    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although the incidence of Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) has been on the rise in recent decades, its etiology is not well understood. Its risk factors are diverse, being both environmental and genetic.  Among the many genetic factors, chromodomain helicase DNA binding protein 8 (CHD8) is one of the most common alterations identified in individuals with ASD. However, the mutations in CHD8 alone are not sufficient to cause ASD, and there are no other single gene causes of autism, suggesting a combinatorial etiology. As such, it is important to study how an environmental challenge may modulate a pre-existing genetic risk. Previous studies have strongly implicated infections in the pregnant mother as one of the most common non-genetic risks. In experimental models, eliciting an immune activation that simulates infection is known as maternal immune activation (MIA). In mice, MIA alone is sufficient to produce behavioral features reminiscent of ASD in offspring.  CHD8 was initially identified due to its role in immune cell regulation, and the present research speculates that mutations in CHD8 may “interact” with MIA to alter neurodevelopmental outcomes. The present research project focuses on validating a mouse model of CHD8 partial loss of function with the intent of using this model with MIA to study combinatorial effects on fetal brain development. Mice were bred to generate offspring with one mutated allele of Chd8 and were challenged with an MIA immunogen (LPS). Experimental offspring trended toward delays in development, increased anxiety, and loss of preference in interacting with a novel social stimulus. These findings emphasize the complex etiology of ASD; it is important to understand how multiple factors produce unique combinatorial phenotypes in the development of this condition.",
